<Base title="Money basics — Career Central">
  <h1>Money basics for your first job</h1>
  <p>
    This is general information, not personal financial advice — giving
    advice tailored to your specific situation legally requires an
    Australian Financial Services Licence, which is exactly why{" "}
    <a href="/">booking a query</a> gets you a person, not just this page.
  </p>

  <h2>Your payslip</h2>
  <p>Every payslip should show, at minimum:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Gross pay (before anything is taken out)</li>
    <li>Tax withheld</li>
    <li>Superannuation contributed on your behalf</li>
    <li>Net pay (what actually lands in your account)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Check it against your actual hours occasionally, especially in your first
    few pay cycles — payroll mistakes happen and are far easier to fix quickly
    than months later.
  </p>

  <h2>Superannuation</h2>
  <p>
    Your employer must pay the Superannuation Guarantee — 12% of your
    ordinary earnings as of the 2025–26 financial year — into a super fund of
    your choice, on top of your pay, whether you're casual, part-time, or
    full-time.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>If you don't nominate a fund, your employer pays into a default one, which may not be the best choice for you.</li>
    <li>Multiple jobs often mean multiple super accounts — each charges its own fees, so consolidating into one fund (via the ATO through myGov) usually saves money.</li>
    <li>Check what insurance (life, income protection) comes bundled with your fund by default — sometimes useful, sometimes not, worth knowing either way.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tax</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Submit your TFN declaration on day one (see <a href="/guides/getting-set-up/">getting set up</a>) so you're not taxed at the top rate by default.</li>
    <li>Australia's tax-free threshold and marginal brackets mean most part-time student income is taxed lightly or not at all — but if you hold two jobs, only claim the tax-free threshold with one employer.</li>
    <li>Lodge a tax return every year (myTax via myGov, or through a registered tax agent) — many students are owed a refund from over-withheld tax.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>HECS-HELP</h2>
  <p>
    Your loan balance is indexed annually and voluntary repayments reduce it —
    worth checking your balance and the current indexation rate via the ATO
    each year rather than assuming it's static.
  </p>

  <h2>Everyday budgeting</h2>
  <p>
    Student income is often irregular — a simple split (a "bills" account you
    don't touch day-to-day, a spending account for everything else) survives
    irregular pay better than tracking every transaction by hand. MoneySmart
    (ASIC's free government resource) has budgeting tools worth using instead
    of a paid app.
  </p>

  <h2>Watch for scams</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The ATO will never ask for payment via gift cards, and won't threaten immediate arrest over the phone.</li>
    <li>Nobody legitimate needs payment to "process" a TFN, ABN, or tax return lodgement beyond a registered tax agent's normal, disclosed fee.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For anything specific to your own payslip, offer, or super fund, that's
    exactly what <a href="/">booking a query</a> is for.
  </p>
</Base>
